# Type System Standards

## Context
- When defining or modifying types/interfaces
- When working with generic types
- When handling API responses
- When integrating with external systems

## Requirements

### Type Definition
- Define explicit types for all variables and functions
- Use strict type checking (`strict: true` in tsconfig)
- Avoid `any` type unless absolutely necessary
- Document type constraints and assumptions
- Use discriminated unions for variant types

### Generic Types
- Define clear constraints on type parameters
- Document type parameter requirements
- Maintain consistent generic parameter naming
- Verify generic type usage across boundaries
- Test with multiple type arguments

### API Integration
- Define complete types for API requests/responses
- Use zod/io-ts for runtime type validation
- Handle partial/undefined data explicitly
- Version types alongside API versions
- Document breaking type changes

### Type Safety
- Use type guards for runtime checks
- Maintain type safety across async boundaries
- Handle nullable values explicitly
- Use branded types for type-level validation
- Implement proper error types

## Examples

### Good Example - Generic Type Safety
```typescript
// Clear type parameter constraints
interface DataProcessor<T extends Record<string, unknown>> {
  process(data: T): Promise<ProcessResult<T>>;
  validate(data: T): T extends Validatable ? boolean : never;
}

// Runtime type checking
const processData = <T extends ApiPayload>(data: T): ProcessResult<T> => {
  // Validate at runtime
  if (!isValidPayload(data)) {
    throw new TypeError('Invalid payload');
  }
  return process(data);
};
```

### Bad Example - Unsafe Type Usage
```typescript
// Don't use any or ignore nullability
const processData = (data: any) => {
  // Unsafe access, no type checking
  return {
    id: data.id,
    value: data.values[0],
  };
};
```

### Good Example - API Types
```typescript
// Version-aware type definitions
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  version: string;
  data: T;
  metadata: ResponseMetadata;
}

// Runtime validation
const validateResponse = <T>(response: unknown): ApiResponse<T> => {
  const schema = responseSchema<T>();
  return schema.parse(response);
};
``` 